WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.786
Welcome to MainFrame.

2
00:00:01.786 --> 00:00:07.461
In about four minutes you will see what a binary search tree is, how to search one,

3
00:00:07.461 --> 00:00:11.769
and how breadth-first search visits every node, level by level.

4
00:00:12.683 --> 00:00:13.965
Here is a tree.

5
00:00:13.965 --> 00:00:19.520
Picture a library catalogue, where every circle is a book number.

6
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:20.973
50 is at the top.

7
00:00:20.973 --> 00:00:22.938
30 and 70 sit below it.

8
00:00:22.938 --> 00:00:26.356
On the bottom row are 20, 40, 60 and 80.

9
00:00:27.283 --> 00:00:30.986
The node at the very top, 50, is called the root.

10
00:00:30.986 --> 00:00:33.405
Every search starts at the root.

11
00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:40.989
30 and 70 hang directly below 50, so they are its children, and 50 is their parent.

12
00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:44.964
20, 40, 60 and 80 have no children.

13
00:00:44.964 --> 00:00:48.533
Nodes with no children are called leaves.

14
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:54.336
No node has more than two children, a left child and a right child.

15
00:00:54.336 --> 00:00:56.524
That makes this a binary tree.

16
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:01.784
A binary search tree adds one rule, and it holds at every node:

17
00:01:01.784 --> 00:01:06.256
smaller values go on the left, and larger values go on the right.

18
00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:08.495
Check it at 50.

19
00:01:08.495 --> 00:01:11.642
30 is smaller, so it is on the left.

20
00:01:11.642 --> 00:01:14.789
70 is larger, so it is on the right.

21
00:01:14.789 --> 00:01:17.673
The same is true at 30 and at 70.

22
00:01:18.583 --> 00:01:20.409
Let's search for 60.

23
00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:23.329
We always start at the root, 50.

24
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.746
60 is larger than 50, so we go right.

25
00:01:27.746 --> 00:01:32.565
The whole left side, 30, 20 and 40, can be ignored.

26
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:37.053
60 is smaller than 70, so we go left.

27
00:01:37.983 --> 00:01:39.286
60 equals 60.

28
00:01:39.286 --> 00:01:44.298
Found it, after only 3 comparisons out of 7 books.

29
00:01:45.217 --> 00:01:48.315
Every comparison throws away part of the tree.

30
00:01:48.315 --> 00:01:53.637
In a well balanced tree with a thousand books, about 10 comparisons are enough.

31
00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.961
Now, breadth-first search, or BFS.

32
00:01:56.961 --> 00:02:02.916
It visits every node of the tree level by level, starting at the top, and going left

33
00:02:02.916 --> 00:02:04.830
to right inside each level.

34
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:09.397
To remember which node is next, BFS keeps a queue.

35
00:02:09.397 --> 00:02:12.460
A queue is like the line at the cafeteria:

36
00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:17.638
whoever joins first is served first, and new arrivals join at the back.

37
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:22.035
Start by putting the root, 50, in the queue.

38
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.842
Take 50 from the front of the queue and visit it.

39
00:02:26.842 --> 00:02:30.971
Its children, 30 and 70, join the back of the queue.

40
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:35.404
Take 30 from the front and visit it.

41
00:02:35.404 --> 00:02:39.219
Its children, 20 and 40, join the back.

42
00:02:39.219 --> 00:02:41.957
The queue is now 70, 20, 40.

43
00:02:42.883 --> 00:02:44.097
Next is 70.

44
00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:48.400
Its children, 60 and 80, join the back.

45
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.931
The queue is now 20, 40, 60, 80.

46
00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:53.658
Visit 20.

47
00:02:53.658 --> 00:02:57.341
It is a leaf, so nothing joins the queue.

48
00:02:58.250 --> 00:02:59.447
Then 40.

49
00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:01.579
Then 60.

50
00:03:02.483 --> 00:03:03.503
And 80.

51
00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:07.875
The queue is empty, so we are done.

52
00:03:07.875 --> 00:03:13.210
The breadth-first order is 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80.

53
00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.174
Level by level, left to right.

54
00:03:17.083 --> 00:03:22.352
BFS visits each node exactly once, so a tree with n nodes takes n steps.

55
00:03:22.352 --> 00:03:28.352
Because it checks the levels nearest the root first, it finds the match closest to

56
00:03:28.352 --> 00:03:29.450
the root first.

57
00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:31.033
To sum up.

58
00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:36.362
A binary search tree keeps smaller values on the left and larger values on the

59
00:03:36.362 --> 00:03:39.163
right, so a search follows just one path.

60
00:03:39.163 --> 00:03:43.740
Breadth-first search uses a queue to visit the tree level by level.

61
00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.062
Now try it yourself in the lesson:

62
00:03:46.062 --> 00:03:48.727
search the tree, then step through BFS.
